# Code conventions

- Interface names begin with `I`.
- Named constants replace magic numbers and repeated literals. Declare them at module scope
  in `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`.
- Declare methods as `const` arrow functions rather than `function` where the language
  allows it.
- Log through the project's existing logger - `logDebug` for diagnostics, `logError` for
  failures - so nothing noisy survives into production. No bare `console.log` in shipped
  code.
- Prefer Bootstrap utility classes over hand-written CSS. Where Bootstrap cannot express
  it, use a CSS module rather than a global stylesheet.
- Extend an existing shared component instead of building a parallel one. When a shared
  component nearly fits, add the missing capability to it.
- Code must pass the linter as written. Never disable, suppress or bypass a rule to make a
  change pass.
- Comments explain a constraint the code cannot express. They do not narrate what the next
  line does, or why a change was made.
- Keep it simple: the straightforward version that a reader understands on first pass beats
  the clever one.
